{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target fails to establish a genuine 'Contradiction' because the two propositions it links are logically compatible under Critical Rationalism. The preference for a better-corroborated theory (proposition A) is a methodological rule based on past error elimination, while the claim that acting on corroboration assumes the future will resemble the past (proposition B) is an epistemological critique of induction. One can consistently hold that we *should* prefer corroborated theories (A) precisely *because* we cannot justify the assumption that the future resembles the past (B), treating the preference as a non-justified conjecture rather than an inductive inference. Since A and B can coexist without logical conflict, the relation 'Contradiction' is false.","successor_question":"If the preference for corroborated theories is not based on an expectation of future success, what normative force does it have for a rational agent?","target":"fb4485fe7513b94d53826b00613d3fefa509b48d01fcbf93d7cf0704615f471f"}]}